Issue: The panel that answers by vote drew at most five replies, and five draws are not enough on the one task whose answer distribution is wide. Replaying the groundable raw replies of the four runs that predate the panel (the prompt, memory and model are byte-identical across them, so every reply is an independent draw of the same question) and voting with the shipped rule over a fixed example set that has eight draws each (30 USPTO / 50 Symptom2Disease / 45 LawBench examples, 1000 random partitions per point) shows the vote still climbing where the cap stops: USPTO 0.1877 at k=3, 0.2291 at k=5, 0.2437 at k=6 and 0.2589 at k=8, against Symptom2Disease 0.8444/0.8456/0.8461/0.8468 and LawBench 0.6045/0.6050/0.6076/0.6107 — so the two extra draws are worth +0.0298 USPTO and +0.0065 elsewhere, while the parent run left the sample's own budget half unspent (USPTO mean 16.2 of the 32 model calls, 337s of the 900s) and 10 of its 60 USPTO samples stopped starting draws at the 450s check. Two further defects were visible in the same traces: 3 USPTO samples returned a single reply because one dead draw ended the panel outright (the exception path breaks the loop), and the fixed 0.5 budget check let one sample run to 817s, close to the runner's 900s wall where the sample would have scored zero.

Modification: solver/agent.py now lets the panel spend the sample's own budget on draws instead of stopping at five. PANEL_SIZE 5 -> 8 (the cap now sits where the call budget, 32 per sample, is the binding limit); PANEL_BUDGET 0.5 -> 0.6 of the sample timeout; a new _out_of_budget guard starts another draw only while the budget is unspent and one more draw of the observed cost (the slowest draw so far) would still fit, so a slow sample stops drawing before the deadline rather than into it; and a draw that dies is a lost draw rather than the end of the panel — the panel keeps drawing on the budget it has and only PANEL_FAILURES = 2 consecutive dead draws end it (the sample's own model-call or deadline limit, which no later draw survives). The unanimity stop at three agreeing replies, the grounding of every reply before it is voted on, and the vote itself are unchanged, and a first draw that fails still propagates so that a broken sample stays an error. The panel loop's semantics were checked with a stub agent (eight checks: unanimous stop, cap, majority, one-failure survival, two-failure stop, budget guard, first-failure propagation) without any model call.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.3167, "Symptom2Disease": 0.85, "LawBench": 0.55, "average": 0.5722, "status": "partial"}, "message": "Expected effect achieved: the extra draws are what the vote gained from. USPTO 0.25 -> 0.3167 (15/60 -> 19/59 scored; the panel draws rose from a mean of 4.3 to 5.78 per sample with 37 of 60 samples now drawing more than five, and on this run's own replies the vote beats its first draw 18/59 against 14/59), while Symptom2Disease 0.86 -> 0.85 and LawBench 0.53 -> 0.55 moved by -0.01 and +0.02 against a counterfactual that predicted +0.0012 and +0.0057 for the extra draws, i.e. inside the run-to-run churn this lineage has measured all along. Average 0.5467 -> 0.5722. Cost is real: model calls 2609 -> 3283 (+26%), input tokens 30.0M -> 35.8M, and 11 USPTO samples now reach the 32-call cap (parent: 1), but the wall clock did not grow (candidate run spans 1513s against the parent's 1738s by trace mtimes) because the new budget guard stops slow panels instead of letting them run into the deadline. Two samples were lost: one USPTO sample whose first draw died on a slow provider call (timeout, 434s, 2 calls) and two LawBench samples to content_filter, all three on the first draw before any vote existed."}

